THE VERIFIER IS DELIBERATELY CARVED OUT
---------------------------------------

`bin/vault-verify.js` is licensed separately under the Apache License 2.0. See
LICENSE-verifier.

This is not generosity, it is the product working as specified. Vault's central
claim is that a customer can prove their record is intact without trusting the
vendor. A verification program the vendor controls cannot support that claim: an
auditor checking a ledger with a proprietary tool is still trusting us. So the
verifier imports nothing from `src/`, can be extracted and published on its own,
and is licensed so that anyone — a customer, their auditor, a regulator, a
competitor — may read it, run it, fork it and satisfy themselves that it does
what it says.

If a future maintainer is tempted to bring the verifier back under the
proprietary licence to protect something: whatever is being protected is worth
less than the claim it would destroy.


SOURCE AVAILABILITY AND ESCROW
------------------------------

The product ships as readable source with no build step and no runtime
dependencies, so a licensed customer already holds everything needed to run it
without the vendor. Escrow arrangements, where a customer requires one, are
addressed in docs/legal/ESCROW.md — including why the usual escrow model is a
weaker guarantee than what this architecture already provides.


